swer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff7e7380-513a-5b76-bdda-1674458cd91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423821} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff39d58c-9bc1-5098-8c5c-72c6dcef5e04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21723135-63f3-5a05-b574-7edf8844e623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423697} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3d17bd-d47b-52b3-8ed1-2de99ff86980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423713} {:answer/id #uuid "51c4f3cd-2606-5c74-b80f-b6abf7dd62e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423717} {:answer/id #uuid "88f8d560-f646-5bd6-b441-ba4e9d5643a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88f8d560-f646-5bd6-b441-ba4e9d5643a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423753} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffe72c59-ed60-54e2-85be-79849b5fc382" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c55065d-263c-577d-a3d6-fb804f092192" :question/math-academy-id "q-363845" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | ${x}_{1}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{4}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a847e10-ad2a-5e19-bffe-4f473d1f4614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407950} {:answer/id #uuid "53b3d1ac-5ba6-5b42-802f-34c001d2a3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407952} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba9bbe0-a4bd-5d08-aca1-3c1661cf6c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407966} {:answer/id #uuid "e36cd36f-f51a-505a-8f73-b691e55977cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407993} {:answer/id #uuid "e71af25d-d057-558a-8db8-29ad46847b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71af25d-d057-558a-8db8-29ad46847b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407996} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3568eb-62d3-583e-93bd-bbecf0a0149d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "256724b0-7532-517f-89d9-acc3daa6cf56" :question/math-academy-id "q-98766" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a220f89c-3d0a-5463-b736-64bfc352bad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399331} {:answer/id #uuid "293c8357-8265-5b01-b470-7d517f25aa72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399332} {:answer/id #uuid "35a514cb-7982-5a31-a007-45f908842ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399333} {:answer/id #uuid "e92e99cd-068f-5ddb-b8d3-1c72fa9118b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399334} {:answer/id #uuid "63e3470d-66bf-5e47-8c08-2675fd0d4713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a220f89c-3d0a-5463-b736-64bfc352bad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399331} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3279ea5-57df-56e5-a59e-a9baf5354ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4670b2c4-578c-543d-913f-4a2e02949175" :question/math-academy-id "q-98768" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
